{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"timid-flattish-upupa","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# J5 — A Dockerized Jira harness as the Data Center verification oracle\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThere is no way to buy a Jira Data Center license to test against: Atlassian ended new DC purchases on\n30 March 2026 and no longer issues DC trial licenses. There is also **no official OpenAPI/Swagger spec for Jira\nDC** — Atlassian publishes only WADL, and the long-standing requests for a spec (JRASERVER-65954,\nJRASERVER-68539) remain open — so spec-driven contract testing is not available either. Cloud's v3 swagger is\nnot a substitute: different auth, ADF vs wiki markup, accountId vs name. Without an oracle, a DC adapter is\nunverifiable except against fakes that encode our own assumptions.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdopt the approach the canonical Python Jira client already uses: **pycontribs/jira runs its integration tests\nagainst a Dockerized Jira Server at `localhost:2990`** (the Atlassian SDK's dev-instance port), with\ndocker-compose plus a health-wait in its `tests/conftest.py`. That instance ships a built-in timebombed license\nfor development and is unaffected by the DC trial shutdown, so it is a license-free real Jira speaking REST v2.\n\nAdd `tests/live_jira_dc/` with a compose file, a readiness probe that polls until the instance answers, and a\npytest fixture that provisions a scratch project and tears down everything it created. The suite is **opt-in**\nvia a pytest marker plus an env flag, so default CI and `make test` are unaffected — matching how rebar already\ngates its live Jira Cloud validation.\n\nPer ADR 0037's live-validation harness pattern, teardown is guaranteed and bilateral: the fixture removes\nremote issues it created and the local ticket rows, so a failed run cannot leave residue that poisons the next.\n\n**Alternative rejected: fixtures and a stateful fake alone.** rebar's own test-design standard allows a\nverified fake for external-service mapping, but a fake verified only against fixtures we recorded cannot\nfalsify assumptions we never thought to record — transitions, permission errors, link direction semantics. The\nfake is still built (J6 uses it for fast unit tests); this story gives it something real to be verified against.\n\n**Alternative rejected: reusing pycontribs/jira's test assertions as the oracle.** Their tests assert their\nclient's behavior, not rebar's field mapping, so they answer a different question. The reusable asset is the\nharness pattern, which is what this story takes.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/live_jira_dc/docker-compose.yml`, `conftest.py`, `README.md` (new).\n- `pyproject.toml` / `pytest.ini` markers — the opt-in marker registration.\n- `Makefile` — a target to bring the instance up and run the marked suite.\n\n## Testing\n\nReal-external-service tier by construction — this story *is* the harness. Its own oracle is that the harness\nstarts, is usable, and cleans up.\n\n- happy path: the harness starts, the readiness probe reports the instance answering REST v2, and a smoke test\n  creates and reads back one issue through raw REST.\n- teardown oracle: after a deliberately failing test, the fixture has still removed every remote issue it\n  created — asserted by querying the project and finding it empty, not by trusting the teardown ran.\n- gating oracle: `make test` with no env flag collects zero tests from `tests/live_jira_dc/`, proving default CI\n  is unaffected.\n- failure path: with the instance down, the marked suite fails with a clear \"harness not running\" message\n  rather than a connection traceback.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `make jira-dc-up` starts the instance and the readiness probe reports ready, output in a ticket comment\n- [ ] a smoke test creates and reads back an issue via REST v2 against it\n- [ ] after an intentionally failing test run, the scratch project contains zero leftover issues\n- [ ] `make test` collects zero tests from `tests/live_jira_dc/` (shown by `pytest --collect-only` counts)\n- [ ] the harness README records the exact Jira version used, so a DC-version drift check has a baseline","id":"c97f-90ae-4976-47c7","parent_id":"e369-a449-4773-48fb","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"J5 — a Dockerized Jira harness as the Data Center verification oracle"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785010236143871001,"uuid":"6bbc1b0b-ded5-4150-9d21-4ab8be5c3f91"}